// Pinned Dash vectors. Provenance, in order of strength:
//
//  * The two mainnet transactions are real chain data. Their on-chain
//    signatures verify against the digest this firmware computes from the same
//    bytes, which is what pins the serialization and the legacy sighash to
//    what Dash actually does rather than to my reading of it.
//  * The self-test request and its signature were produced offline with an
//    independent RFC 6979 implementation and are reproduced here as fixed
//    bytes; the device has to match them with mbedtls, so the two
//    implementations have to agree on every byte.
//
// The file is data only: nothing in the build regenerates it, and every value
// in it is checked by run_dash_self_tests() / run_dash_signature_self_test().
//
// The two mainnet vectors are real chain data: the digest below is the
// one their on-chain signature verifies against, so the firmware
// serialization is pinned to money that moved, not to a recollection of
// how Dash Core is written. The third vector is a request this wallet
// can actually build; its signature was produced with RFC 6979 and has
// to come out identical on the device.

